    "seo": {
      "title": "Digital Newsletters About Iran | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Explore Iran-focused newsletters, their editorial models, and the differences between curated reading, expert analysis, and newsroom email services."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "editorial-models",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Different ways of reading Iran",
        "content": "An Iran-focused newsletter can be a named editor’s selection of important developments, a vehicle for specialist essays, or an email service attached to a newsroom. The inspected subscription pages illustrate these distinct models: The Iranist emphasizes individual curation, Iran360 combines analysis with a curated email, and Radio Farda offers a newsroom newsletter subscription. These are examples rather than a ranking or a census of the field. [s1][s2][s3]\n\nFor an international reader, the distinction matters because selection and original reporting are different editorial activities. A useful reading question is whether an issue presents newly gathered evidence, interprets existing material, or directs readers to reporting elsewhere. A publication can do more than one of these things; its subscription pitch alone cannot establish the balance.",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "individual-curation",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "The named curator",
        "content": "The Iranist identifies Holly Dagres as its curator and says the newsletter began in 2015. Its About page describes a remit extending beyond security and nuclear-policy headlines to ordinary life, activists, social-media trends, and human rights. It advertises a Friday email. These statements establish the publication’s declared identity and intended coverage, rather than independently demonstrating its reach or the regularity of delivery. [s1]\n\nThe appeal of this model is editorial continuity: readers can assess how a particular curator selects and frames material across successive issues. The corresponding responsibility is to distinguish the curator’s interpretation from the evidence in linked reporting. A confident introduction to an article is not a substitute for reading that article’s sourcing.",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "essays-and-newsroom-email",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Expert essays and newsroom distribution",
        "content": "Iran360 describes itself as a group of Iran experts covering society, economics, culture, and politics. Its About page says it publishes analysis on its website and sends subscribers a selected expert essay and curated newsletter weekly. It also links to publication archives. This makes the relationship between the email and the website explicit: the inbox is one route into a larger body of material. Claims about the distinctiveness or quality of that analysis remain the publisher’s own assessment. [s2]\n\nRadio Farda’s English subscription page presents a different arrangement. It requests an email address and consent for RFE/RL, Radio Free Europe/Radio Liberty, to use that address to send selected newsletters. The inspected form does not specify a delivery schedule. It supports identifying an offered subscription service, but not claiming that a particular issue was delivered or that the service had a measured readership. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s2", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "reading-and-evidence",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Read the issue as well as the promise",
        "content": "These examples should be approached through their published work as well as their introductory pages. Compare the date of an email with the dates of the material it discusses; identify which passages are reporting and which are commentary; and follow consequential claims to their underlying evidence. For a curated publication, the choice of what to include deserves attention alongside the accuracy of individual summaries.\n\nThe present evidence establishes several publishing models, but it does not support conclusions about newsletters’ popularity inside Iran, their representation of provincial perspectives, or the relative importance of Persian-language and English-language services. Establishing those questions requires dated issue samples and audience evidence. The examples here provide a starting framework for reading the medium, not a complete account of its market. [s1][s2][s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3"]
      }
    ],
